{
  "dom_id": "step-q70467",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Find the roots of the polynomial $p(z)=18{z}^{4}+3{z}^{3}-3{z}^{2}.$",
  "worked_solution": "To find the roots of the polynomial, we must solve the equation $p(z)=0,$ that is\n\n$$\n18{z}^{4}+3{z}^{3}-3{z}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $3{z}^{2}.$ We can factor out the $3{z}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{2}(6{z}^{2}+z-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $6{z}^{2}+z-1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{2}(6{z}^{2}+z-1) & =0 \\\\ 3{z}^{2}(3z-1)(2z+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $z=0,$ $z=\\frac{1}{3},$ and $z=-\\frac{1}{2}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
